# Line endings, declared once for the whole repository.
#
# CRLF everywhere, because that is what this project's own tooling already
# writes and what .editorconfig already declares for C#:
#   src/mpvw-app/.editorconfig          end_of_line = crlf
#   tools/check-icon-standard.py        newline="\r\n"   (--update-baseline)
#   tools/check-typography-standard.py  newline="\r\n"   (--update-baseline)
#   tools/gen-mpv-options.py            newline="\r\n"   (generated JSON)
#
# Declaring it here rather than relying on a developer's core.autocrlf means the
# blob in the index, the working tree, and every `git diff` agree. Without this
# file the index held LF while the working tree held CRLF, which is invisible in
# a diff and only surfaces when someone else clones.
#
# `text=auto eol=crlf` also means binary detection is by content, not by this
# file guessing extensions -- but the explicit `-text` block below pins the
# binaries that matter, so a font whose header happens to look like text can
# never be normalised.

* text=auto eol=crlf

# --- Binary: never touched, never diffed --------------------------------
*.png    -text
*.jpg    -text
*.jpeg   -text
*.gif    -text
*.ico    -text
*.ttf    -text
*.otf    -text
*.exe    -text
*.dll    -text
*.lib    -text
*.winmd  -text
*.nupkg  -text
*.zip    -text

# --- Exception: a real bash script --------------------------------------
# tools/deploy-test.sh runs under bash (it calls Unix `find` and `cp`). A CRLF
# line ending makes bash treat the trailing \r as part of each command, so the
# script fails on its first line. It is the only POSIX script in the tree;
# everything else is PowerShell, which reads either ending.
tools/deploy-test.sh  text eol=lf
